WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.310 --> 00:00:07.470
In theory, there are only four styles of parenting.

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:13.730
Authoritarian parents are controlling and
demand obedience without considering the child’s

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:14.990
point of view.

00:00:14.990 --> 00:00:19.410
Permissive parents are loving, but don’t
exert any control.

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:22.000
There are no rules.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.900
Authoritative parents who are firm but loving.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:29.050
They encourage independence, within limits.

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:34.910
Neglecting parents are uninvolved and often
uninterested in their own child.

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:41.050
Recently a 5th style was proposed, but we
will come back to that later.

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:47.910
The styles range from controlling and demanding,
to complete freedom; and from cold and unresponsive

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:50.140
to loving and receptive.

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:57.620
Each, authoritative, permissive, authoritarian
and neglectful parents, have their place.

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:02.460
To understand what it means to grow up with
parents from each end of the spectrum, we

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:07.700
can imagine the lives of four children.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:10.280
Sara's parents are Authoritarian.

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:15.729
They love their girl, but they believe that
strict rules are important to make Sara become

00:01:15.729 --> 00:01:19.289
well behaved and fit into the society.

00:01:19.289 --> 00:01:22.049
If Sara cries, she’s told to stop.

00:01:22.049 --> 00:01:26.409
If she talks back, she is sent to the corner
for timeout.

00:01:26.409 --> 00:01:31.509
If she forgets to take care of her chores
around the house, she is not allowed to play

00:01:31.509 --> 00:01:35.210
with her toys.

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:40.189
Sara learns that suppressing her emotions
and following her duties is the way to get

00:01:40.189 --> 00:01:41.819
through the day.

00:01:41.819 --> 00:01:47.630
To get the love of her parents, and to avoid
making them upset, she becomes obedient.

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:54.189
However, because Sara was never allowed to
decide for herself or follow her intrinsic

00:01:54.189 --> 00:01:58.920
interests, as an adult, she doesn't know what
she really wants.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:04.159
She begins living a life that seems perfect
to her parents and society, but might leave

00:02:04.159 --> 00:02:08.360
her unhappy on the inside.

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:14.099
Permissive parents , like those of Peter,
love their little boy so much that they believe

00:02:14.099 --> 00:02:20.620
they should fulfill all his wishes, give him
full freedom and never say “no”.

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:25.830
Peter enjoys full control over his parents
and gets whatever he wants.

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:28.640
If he doesn’t want to walk, he’ll be carried.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:31.940
If he wants ice-cream, ice-cream he gets.

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:37.840
If he wants to play games, he’ll be playing
them all night.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:42.879
Peter grows up completely without borders
and does whatever he thinks is right.

00:02:42.879 --> 00:02:48.819
He never coped with conflict and he did not
learn to control his emotions.

00:02:48.819 --> 00:02:53.400
The fact that he always got what he wanted
made him a bad loser.

00:02:53.400 --> 00:03:00.879
As he grows older he often acts inconsiderate
and doesn’t know his limits.

00:03:00.879 --> 00:03:06.099
Arthur's Authoritative parents respect their
child's needs, but believe that kids need

00:03:06.099 --> 00:03:08.519
freedom within certain limits.

00:03:08.519 --> 00:03:13.720
Arthur can freely play, but when he’s done,
he needs to help tidy up.

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:18.000
He is allowed to eat ice cream, but only on
Sundays.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:21.650
Screen-time is limited to 30 minutes a day.

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:27.000
There may be conflict, but the parents listen
to what Arthur has to say and then lay down

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:28.000
the rules.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:30.310
However, they don’t give in, nor do they
use rewards or punishments.

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:34.730
Arthur learns that some things are difficult,
but his parents give him all the support he

00:03:34.730 --> 00:03:36.600
needs to get through it.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:42.769
He develops the strength to endure hardships
and to continue following his interests and

00:03:42.769 --> 00:03:44.040
passions.

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:49.400
In class he bravely expresses his own opinions
in an appropriate manner.

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:53.970
During breaks he can show his emotions and
act freely.

00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:59.189
As an adult he agrees to rules only after
they have been discussed and he feels like

00:03:59.189 --> 00:04:03.319
he understands them.

00:04:03.319 --> 00:04:07.440
Neglectful parents are usually not present
in their children's life.

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:10.999
Nora often feels completely alone in the world.

00:04:10.999 --> 00:04:16.299
She experiences full freedom to do anything
she wants to and has plenty of imagination

00:04:16.299 --> 00:04:22.669
, but she never receives any feedback, affection,
love or even attention.

00:04:22.669 --> 00:04:27.830
Nora realizes that it doesn’t matter what
she does, because no one cares anyway.

00:04:27.830 --> 00:04:32.660
The lack of attention, leads to a lack of
trust in herself and others.

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:38.280
She becomes insecurely attached, unable to
form healthy relationships and develops a

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:40.810
negative image of herself.

00:04:40.810 --> 00:04:48.080
To stop feeling unworthy of love , she tries
not to feel anything at all.

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:54.160
In recent years, over-Involved parents, who
are present in every aspect of their child’s

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:57.960
life, are often referred to as the 5th style.

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:04.099
These parents are also known as ‘snow plows’,
removing obstacles out of their kids' path,

00:05:04.099 --> 00:05:10.449
or 'helicopter' parents, who hover about and
micro-manage every aspect of their child's

00:05:10.449 --> 00:05:11.650
life.

00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:16.740
Since they won’t let their children do anything
alone, the children can’t learn to overcome

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:19.340
challenges by themselves.

00:05:19.340 --> 00:05:25.120
Research infers that these children don’t
like to solve difficult problems , lack perseverance

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:32.460
and may even procrastinate in protest when
something requires a lot of effort.

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:39.259
Since most research on parenting is based
on self-reports and was done in the US and

00:05:39.259 --> 00:05:45.120
Europe, it is not clear how strong the observed
effects will hold up in controlled observational

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:50.620
experiments or in other parts of the word.

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:56.310
The four parenting styles were first introduced
by Psychologist Diana Baumrind.

00:05:56.310 --> 00:06:01.160
For good parenting she advised: “balance
of demandingness and responsiveness".

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:07.720
Add to that the wise words of Maria Montessori
to “Never help a child with a task at which

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:09.720
he feels he can succeed.”

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:12.229
and parents should probably do pretty well.

00:06:12.229 --> 00:06:15.349
What do you think?

00:06:15.349 --> 00:06:20.979
Should parents resort to a specific style
or should they decide what's best to do in

00:06:20.979 --> 00:06:26.280
a given situation — as long as they don’t
neglect or abuse their child.

